| footnotes = {{cnote|A|The party is also described as [[Left-conservatism|left-conservative]],<ref name="left_con">{{bulleted list|{{cite thesis |title=The Missing Quadrant: Left-conservative parties and voters in Western Europe |degree=Master in Political Science |first=Michael |last=Souer |publisher=Universiteit Leiden |url=https://studenttheses.universiteitleiden.nl/handle/1887/3160270 |year=2020 |page=14 |quote="In 2006, before these developments, Greece had two parties positioned in the leftconservative quadrant; the Communist Party of Greece (KKE) and the Democratic Social Movement (DIKKI), a split-off of PASOK, both combined a far-left economic position with a mildly culturally conservative position. While DIKKI has since descended into obscurity, Figure 4 reveals that the KKE has in fact moved further right on the sociocultural dimension, from a 6.1 to a 7."}}|{{cite web |url=https://www.pressenza.com/2023/06/national-elections-in-greece-conservatism-and-the-far-rightwin/ |title=National elections in Greece: Conservatism and the far right win |date=28 June 2023 |website=Pressenza Athens |location=Athens |quote="Only the conservative left, expressed by the KKE, emerged stronger, while MeRA25 was left out of parliament."}}|{{cite thesis |title=Far-Left Party Family in Europe: The socioeconomic factors of far-left voting |location=Athens |year=2020 |first=Maria |last=Kostopoulou |publisher=Athens University of Economics and Business |degree=Bachelor |page=15 |quote="The Communist Party of Greece* (KKE) classified as a conservative extremist far-left party and the Syriza classified as a radical far-left party until 2015."}}}}</ref> or left-authoritarian,<ref>{{cite journal |title=Bündnis Sahra Wagenknecht (BSW): Left-Wing Authoritarian—and Populist? An Empirical Analysis |first=Jan Philipp |last=Thomeczek |year=2024 |journal=Politische Vierteljahresschrift |volume=65 |issue=1 |page=552 |publisher=Springer |doi=10.1007/s11615-024-00544-z |quote="According to the Chapel Hill Expert Survey (Bakker et al. 2019), there are several parties with a similar left-authoritarian profile in Europe. Examples are the Social Democratic Party in Romania (PSD), the Bulgarian Socialist Party (BSP), and far-left parties such as the Communist Party of Greece (KKE) and the Communist Party of Bohemia and Moravia (KSČM). Furthermore, a similar profile is found in the Law and Justice Party (PiS) in Poland (Hillen and Steiner 2020, p. 344), which led the government for almost 20 years."|hdl=10419/315570 |hdl-access=free }}</ref> given its conservative stances on social issues.<ref name="all_ze_cons">{{bulleted list|{{cite book |title=The Palgrave Handbook of Radical Left Parties in Europe |editor1=Fabien Escalona |year=2023 |isbn=978-1-137-56264-7 |doi=10.1057/978-1-137-56264-7 |publisher=Palgrave Macmillan |chapter=Conclusion |first1=Daniel |last1=Keith |first2=Luke |last2=March |author-link2=:es:Luke March |page=703 |quote=However, most of the parties from these subgroups promote conservative social policies e.g. regarding the teaching of national history. For example, the KKE opposed same-sex civil partnerships (Polenta, 2016). Criticisms of the EU can also be anchored in nationalism (e.g. PCP). As Luke March shows in Chapter 20, in ECE, RLPs often promote nationalist, sexist and homophobic positions.}}|{{cite web |url=https://gay45.eu/gay-greece/ |title=The Cradle of Queer Civilisation? Why Gays Still Love Greece |date=23 February 2024 |first=Danny |last=Tye |quote="NPR reported that political opposition to the bill was present within the Hellenic Parliament, though limited to small far-right parties and the socially conservative Communist Party." |website=[[Gay 45]]}}|{{cite journal |url=https://hrcak.srce.hr/clanak/394167 |title=After the Storm: Party Systems in Southern Europe in the Wake of the Eurocrisis |journal=Suvremeni Mediteran |volume=1 |issue=1 |year=2022 |first=Višeslav |last=Raos |doi=10.17818/SM/2021/1.2 |via=[[:hr:Hrčak (portal)|Hrčak]] |page=28 |quote="The rather unusual far-right outliers for the Greek party system in the 2000s pertain to the Greek Communist Party (KKE), an orthodox Marxist-Leninist party with underpinnings of social conservatism."|doi-access=free }}|{{cite web |url=https://www.semafor.com/article/11/22/2024/conservative-leftists-want-to-upset-the-political-spectrum |title='Conservative leftists' want to upset the political spectrum |date=22 November 2024 |first=Brad |last=Glasser |website=[[Semafor (website)|Semafor]] |quote="BSW has been placed in a tradition of “Old Left” parties that retain the social conservatism of the Soviet era. Communist and social democratic parties across Europe fall into this vein, like the Communist Party of Greece, which has campaigned against same-sex marriage in the country."}}|{{cite web |title=Why the left failed |first=Michael |last=Chessum |website=[[The New World (British newspaper)|The New World]] |date=16 February 2025 |url=https://www.thenewworld.co.uk/michael-chessum-why-the-left-failed/ |quote="The Greek Communist Party (KKE), for instance, has long combined strong leftist economic policies with socially conservative views, including opposition to gay marriage."}}|{{cite web |url=https://carta-politica.webnode.page/l/%28re%29-elecciones-griegas%3A-%C2%BFotra-mayoria-absoluta-para-la-derecha/ |title=(Re) Elecciones Griegas: ¿Otra mayoría absoluta para la derecha? |date=24 June 2023 |language=es |website=Carta Política |quote="Asimismo, la presencia del KKE, el cual no solo es socialmente conservador, sino también pro - ruso, rompe casi todas las posibilidades de esta unidad, pues muchos dirigentes de este partido prefieren la continuación de ND antes que un gobierno progresista." |trans-quote="Furthermore, the presence of the KKE, which is not only socially conservative but also pro-Russian, undermines almost all possibilities for this unity, as many leaders of this party prefer the continuation of ND to a progressive government."}}|{{cite journal |title=Índice del Populismo Autoritario |date=April 2024 |first1=Andreas Johansson |last1=Heinö |author-link1=:sv:Andreas Johansson Heinö |language=es |journal=Timbro |url=https://www.epicenternetwork.eu/publications/timbro-authoritarian-populism-index-2024/ |publisher=Swedish Free Enterprise Foundation |page=169 |quote="Además de estar muy a la izquierda en cuestiones económicas, el KKE está en contra de la pertenencia de Grecia a la OTAN y a la UE, y adopta posturas conservadoras en cuestiones sociales." |trans-quote="In addition to being far to the left on economic issues, the KKE is against the Greek membership in NATO and the EU and takes conservative positions on social issues."}}|{{cite thesis |title=O marxismo-leninismo e a crise económica e financeira de 2008: oportunidades e desafios em análise |url=https://repositorium.sdum.uminho.pt/bitstream/1822/61136/1/Jose_%2BPedro%2BMartins%2BLopes.pdf |language=pt |first=José Pedro |last=Martins Lopes |publisher=Universidade do Minho |degree=Mestrado em Ciência Política |page=90 |date=April 2019 |quote="Demonstra-se nesta dissertação que a crise trouxe um aumento do ceticismo face à integração europeia para ambos os partidos, que o KKE se tornou mais autoritário no pós-crise e o PCP mais nacionalista. Os comunistas gregos mantêm-se mais à esquerda e mais eurocéticos do que os portugueses. PCP e KKE radicalizaram ligeiramente o seu posicionamento na dimensão esquerda-direita económica e não só se tornaram um pouco mais céticos face à integração europeia, como aumentaram a saliência desta dimensão nas suas posições políticas. O posicionamento de ambos face às new politics não registou alterações significativas, especialmente no que toca ao conservadorismo social." |trans-quote="This dissertation demonstrates that the crisis has led to increased scepticism towards European integration for both parties, with the KKE becoming more authoritarian in the post-crisis period and the PCP becoming more nationalist. The Greek communists remain further to the left and more Eurosceptic than their Portuguese counterparts. The PCP and KKE have slightly radicalised their position on the economic left-right dimension and have not only become a little more sceptical about European integration, but have also increased the prominence of this dimension in their political positions. The position of both parties on new politics has not changed significantly, especially with regard to their social conservatism."}}}}The party is also considered [[cultural conservatism|culturally conservative]]:<br>{{bulleted list|{{cite book |title=The Oxford Handbook of Modern Greek Politics |chapter=The Communists |first=Giorgos |last=Charalambous |date=2020 |publisher=Oxford University Press |isbn=978-0-19-882510-4 |edition=1st |editor1=Kevin Featherstone |editor2=Dimitri A. Sotiropoulos |page=324 |quote="Moreover, Greek communism is pervaded by a cultural conservatism, most evident in the KKE’s adamantly negative position on soft drugs and its recent intervention concerning the (SYRIZA) Government’s proposed legislation on same-sex marriage (the Co-habitation Agreement)."}}|{{cite journal |url=https://library.fes.de/pdf-files/bueros/athen/21157.pdf |title=The Greek Party Landscape in 2023 |journal=Democracy and Human Rights Policy Brief |publisher=Friedrich-Ebert-Stiftung |quote="By looking at the distribution of KKE voters’ answers on each of the 30 issues included in the What2Vote questionnaire, we are able to identify the main sources of party–voter incongruence. On the cultural axis, much of the incongruence results from the party’s conservative stances on issues." |first=Emmanouil |last=Tsatsanis |editor1=Lamprini Rori |editor2=Vasiliki Georgiadou |editor3=Dimitri A. Sotiropoulos |editor4=Eftychia Teperoglou |editor5=Yannis Tsirbas |editor6=Lamprini Rori |year=2024 |page=11}}|{{cite thesis |title=The Missing Quadrant: Left-conservative parties and voters in Western Europe |degree=Master in Political Science |first=Michael |last=Souer |publisher=Universiteit Leiden |url=https://studenttheses.universiteitleiden.nl/handle/1887/3160270 |year=2020 |page=14 |quote="In 2006, before these developments, Greece had two parties positioned in the leftconservative quadrant; the Communist Party of Greece (KKE) and the Democratic Social Movement (DIKKI), a split-off of PASOK, both combined a far-left economic position with a mildly culturally conservative position. While DIKKI has since descended into obscurity, Figure 4 reveals that the KKE has in fact moved further right on the sociocultural dimension, from a 6.1 to a 7."}}|{{cite thesis |title='What do you want the world to be like in the future?' Utopian ethnography in the crisis-ordinary of late 2010s Pátra, Greece |year=2020 |first=Judy |last=Thorne |degree=Doctor of Philosophy in the Faculty of Humanities |publisher=University of Manchester |quote="Cóstas point blank refused to attend the party’s summer cultural festival, at which the ageing Greek rockstar Vasilis Papakonstantinou headlined, to an enormous crowd of people who my KKE informants pointed out to me were not communists but who had just showed up for the free concert. Cóstas told me that this kind of crowd-pleasing was how the party justified its cultural conservatism." |url=https://research.manchester.ac.uk/en/studentTheses/what-do-you-want-the-world-to-be-like-in-the-future-utopian-ethno}}}}</ref>}}
}}
{{Communist Party of Greece}}
The '''Communist Party of Greece''',{{efn|{{langx|el|Κομμουνιστικό Κόμμα Ελλάδας|Kommounistikó Kómma Elládas}}, '''KKE'''}} also known by its acronym '''KKE''', is a [[Marxism–Leninism|Marxist–Leninist]] [[List of political parties in Greece|political party in Greece]].<ref name=Nordsieck>{{cite web|url=http://parties-and-elections.eu/greece.html|title=Greece|last=Nordsieck|first=Wolfram|date=2019|website=Parties and Elections in Europe|access-date=22 April 2018|archive-date=26 January 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180126022304/http://www.parties-and-elections.eu/greece.html|url-status=live}}</ref> It was founded in 1918 as the '''Socialist Workers' Party of Greece''' ('''SEKE'''){{efn|Greek: Σοσιαλιστικό Εργατικό Κόμμα Ελλάδος – '''''S'''osialistikó '''E'''rgatikó '''K'''ómma '''E'''lládos''. After the party's admission to the Communist International in 1920, it changed its full name to '''Socialist Workers' Party of Greece (Communist)''' ('''SEKE (K)''').}} and adopted its current name in November 1924.<ref name="newsbeast">{{Cite web|last=Kyrkos|first=Vaggelis|date=3 November 2018|title=Ποιοι ήταν αυτοί που ίδρυσαν το ΚΚΕ [Who were founders of the KKE]|url=https://www.newsbeast.gr/weekend/arthro/4157041/poioi-itan-aytoi-poy-idrysan-to-kke|url-status=live|access-date=|website=Newsbeast|language=el|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181104004301/https://www.newsbeast.gr/weekend/arthro/4157041/poioi-itan-aytoi-poy-idrysan-to-kke |archive-date=4 November 2018}}</ref> It is the oldest [[political party]] in modern [[Politics of Greece|Greek politics]] to still be active.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bollier |first=Sam |date=1 May 2012 |title=A guide to Greece's political parties |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2012/5/1/a-guide-to-greeces-political-parties |url-status=live |access-date=|website= [[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]] |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210709181927/https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2012/5/1/a-guide-to-greeces-political-parties |archive-date=9 July 2021}}</ref>

The party was banned in 1936, but played a significant role in the [[Greek resistance]] and the [[Greek Civil War]], and its membership peaked in the mid-1940s. Legalization of the KKE was restored following the fall of the [[Greek Junta]] in 1974. The party has achieved appointing [[Hellenic Parliament|MPs]] in all elections since its restoration in 1974, and took part in a coalition government in 1989 when it got more than 13% of the vote. Ever since the 13th Congress of the KKE in 1991, the party has rejected "participation in and support of bourgeois governments", claiming the restoration of its revolutionary characteristics<ref>{{cite web |last1=Marinos |first1=Giorgos |title=Parliamentary politics and the communist party: the electoral arena, experience and lessons from Greece and the KKE |url=https://inter.kke.gr/en/m-article/75b96f30-bb2b-11f0-b1e1-be037ce642f9/ |website=Communist Party of Greece |access-date=25 April 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Papariga |first1=Aleka |title=Η στρατηγική σημασία της άρνησης συμμετοχής του ΚΚΕ σε αστική κυβέρνηση (2012-2015) |url=https://www.komep.gr/m-article/I-stratigiki-simasia-tis-arnisis-symmetoxis-toy-KKE-se-astiki-kybernisi-2012-2015/ |website=Κομμουνιστική Επιθεώρηση |access-date=25 April 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Political Bureau |first1=Central Committee of the KKE |title=Η πορεία αποκατάστασης του επαναστατικού χαρακτήρα του ΚΚΕ |url=https://www.komep.gr/m-article/I-POREIA-APOKATASTASIS-TOY-EPANASTATIKOY-XARAKTIRA-TOY-KKE |website=Κομμουνιστική Επιθεώρηση |access-date=20 August 2026 |language=Greek |date=2013 |trans-title=The course of restoring the revolutionary character of the KKE}}</ref> and denouncing the coalition government of 1989.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Παπαρήγα |first1=Αλέκα |title=Δύο ριζικά διαφορετικές επιλογές για τη συγκρότηση κυβέρνησης στο έδαφος του καπιταλισμού, 1989-90 και 2012 |url=https://www.komep.gr/m-article/Do-rizika-diaforetikes-epiloges-gia-ti-sygkrotisi-kybernisis-sto-edafos-toy-kapitalismo-1989-90-kai-2012/ |website=Κομμουνιστική Επιθεώρηση |access-date=25 April 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Αλέκα Παπαρήγα: Αποτιμώντας το «'89» και το «όχι» στον ΣΥΡΙΖΑ |url=https://www.kathimerini.gr/politics/563633890/aleka-papariga-apotimontas-to-89-kai-to-ochi-ston-syriza/ |website=Η Καθημερινή |access-date=25 April 2026}}</ref>

The KKE participates in the [[International Meeting of Communist and Workers' Parties]] (IMCWP) and the [[European Communist Action]] (ECA).<ref>{{cite web |date=27 November 2023 |title=European Communist Action's Founding Declaration |url=https://www.idcommunism.com/2023/11/european-communist-actions-founding-declaration.html?m=1 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231202090141/https://www.idcommunism.com/2023/11/european-communist-actions-founding-declaration.html?m=1 |archive-date=2 December 2023 |access-date=1 December 2023}}</ref>

The KKE describes itself as a revolutionary party that wants to build a socialist-communist society through the [[dictatorship of the proletariat]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Statutes of the KKE |url=https://inter.kke.gr/.galleries/statutesKKE/StatuteEn.pdf |website=Communist Party of Greece |access-date=20 August 2026 |date=11-14 April 2013 |quote=The strategic goal of the KKE is the conquest of revolutionary working class power, the dictatorship of the proletariat, for socialist construction.}}</ref> In addition to being a [[Communism|communist]] party, it has also been described as [[Stalinist]],<ref name="stalinist"/> [[Nationalism|nationalist]] or [[Left-wing nationalism|left-wing nationalist]],<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Halikiopoulou |first1=Daphne |last2=Vasilopoulou |first2=Sofia |last3=Nanou |first3=Kyriaki |date=19 July 2010 |title=The Paradox of Nationalism: The Common Denominator of Radical Left and Radical Right Euroscepticism |url=https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=1643722 |website=SSRN |ssrn=1643722 |quote=α) «Although KKE is not an anti-immigrant party (KKE, 2009: 14), it is still a party whose rhetoric is strongly nationalistic.» β) «Similarly to the KKE, this radical left nationalism can be understood as anti-imperialist, anti-collaborationist, anti-Western and economically isolationist.}}</ref> [[Economic nationalism|economic nationalist]],<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Halikiopoulou |first1=Daphne |last2=Vasilopoulou |first2=Sofia |last3=Nanou |first3=Kyriaki |date=19 July 2010 |title=The Paradox of Nationalism: The Common Denominator of Radical Left and Radical Right Euroscepticism |url=https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=1643722 |website=SSRN |ssrn=1643722 |quote=α) The party suggests that the only economic route for Greece is one of self-sustenance which would entail complete nationalisation of the means of production and a return to more traditional means of economic development such as agriculture of specifically Greek products (KKE, 2007: 12). β)) «KKE's economic nationalism equates the EU with globalisation and globalisation with capitalism. The party suggests that the only economic route for Greece is one of self-sustenance which would entail complete nationalisation of the means of production and a return to more traditional means of economic development such as agriculture of specifically Greek products (KKE, 2007: 12).»}}</ref> [[socially conservative]],<ref name="soc_con"/> [[culturally conservative]],<ref name="cult_con"/> and [[Anti-imperialism|anti-imperialist]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Political parties of Greece |url=https://archive.wikiwix.com/cache/index2.php?url=https://www.fes-athens.org/projekte/political-parties-of-greece/#federation=archive.wikiwix.com&tab=url |access-date=2025-06-21 |website=archive.wikiwix.com |language=de}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Eleftheriou |first=Costas |date=2015-01-01 |title=Greek anti-imperialism: contemporary era |url=https://www.academia.edu/43084048 |journal=Cope, Z. And I. Ness (Eds), Palgrave Encyclopedia of Imperialism and Anti-Imperialism, Basingstoke: Palgrave/Macmillan, Pp. 341-346}}</ref>

